            336,
            370,
            190
          ],
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Why not warp the noise like an image? Top left, a roller coaster zooming forward. Top right, nearest-neighbor copies the same noise pixel into streaks. Bottom left, bilinear averages neighbors: average four unit-variance values and variance drops as low as one quarter, so it goes gray, and neighbors share inputs, so it goes blurry. Moran's I, the spatial correlation, is 0.26 and 0.30, p of zero. Ours is 0.0001, p 0.84, same as fresh noise. And the model notices: in DeepFloyd super-resolution, bilinear noise doubles LPIPS, 0.29 to 0.60."
    },
    {
      "title": "Noise as particles",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_gwtf/algo_graph_fixed.png",
          "rect": [
            222,
            84,
            516,
            343
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "X_i = q/d + (Z_i − S/d)/√d",
          "rect": [
            180,
            432,
            600,
            34
          ],
          "size": 24,
          "color": "#000000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Pieces are jointly Gaussian with zero covariance, so independent.",
          "rect": [
            80,
            474,
            800,
            36
          ],
          "size": 24,
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "So how? Treat each noise pixel as a particle. Green arrows push particles forward along the flow; three land on q-prime-two. The orange pixels are holes nobody landed on, so blue arrows pull from q-two along the backward flow. Now q-two has three edges, contraction included, so we split it into three pieces with this formula from How I Warped Your Noise: q over d, plus fresh noise with its mean removed. The pieces are jointly Gaussian with zero covariance, so they're independent, and each is used exactly once. Each target sums its pieces and renormalizes, so if frame t is i.i.d. Gaussian, so is t plus one. The red p is density. It's not for Gaussianity; it keeps merged regions weighted right over long videos. It's linear in pixels, 2 milliseconds a frame, 26 times faster than How I Warped Your Noise."
    },
