        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "DL3DV camera: mIoU 0.83 vs 0.71 (MotionClone)",
          "rect": [
            30,
            430,
            900,
            32
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#000000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Plain CogVideoX, no warped noise: mIoU 0.52; ours 0.74",
          "rect": [
            30,
            468,
            900,
            32
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Why fine-tune? Untuned CogVideoX roughly follows warped noise, but frames fall apart. Each frame is still Gaussian, but across time it deliberately isn't, and it's never seen that. So we warp noise in pixel space for four million videos, pool it down to the latent, and train a rank-2048 LoRA, 40 A100-days. No new modules: the LoRA merges back in, so zero extra inference cost. At inference you pick gamma, how much fresh noise to mix in. Lower is stricter: gamma 0.5 gives tracking mIoU 0.74, gamma 0.9 only 0.50. Top row is input motion, bottom is ours: cut-and-drag, camera transfer, a 3D blob into a squirrel. On VIPSeg object motion we beat SG-I2V and MotionClone on both FID and mIoU, same story on DL3DV camera, and plain CogVideoX drops to 0.52. In a 40-person study, ours won 82% on cut-and-drag and 90% on turntables."
